## The short answer

Prospect manually while you are still learning who buys and which message earns a reply. Hand it to an agency once the approach works and your own time, not your idea, is what limits how much of it gets done. The mistake is outsourcing the experiments: an agency can run a proven process, but it cannot tell you what to sell.

## What manual LinkedIn prospecting really involves

Done properly, it is more than sending connection requests. Someone has to decide who is worth contacting, write a note that does not read like a template, follow up without being pushy, answer every reply, and keep track of where each conversation stands. Done by the founder, it has real advantages: the voice is genuine, you hear objections first-hand, and you learn fast.

The weakness is consistency. Manual prospecting is the first thing to slip in a busy week, and an outreach programme that runs for three days and stops for ten rarely builds momentum.

## What an agency changes

An agency replaces your time with a team and a process: research on who to contact, personalised connection requests and follow-ups, reply handling, content that makes your profile credible, and reporting on what is working. At Myntmore that is a dedicated growth team and AI agents, with activity kept within LinkedIn's limits, and about 90 minutes a month of your own input.

The trade-offs are real. You give up some hands-on control, you need to brief the team well, and the work is only as good as the ICP and message it starts from. An agency is a fee instead of your time, so it only pays off if your time is worth more elsewhere.

## Side by side

{% table %}
* Factor
* Manual prospecting
* LinkedIn outreach agency
---
* Your time
* Daily, and it competes with selling, product and hiring.
* A short monthly input plus approvals; the team does the daily work.
---
* Consistency
* Depends on your week. Easy to let slip.
* Runs on a schedule regardless of how busy you are.
---
* Targeting depth
* Limited by how much research you can do by hand.
* Systematic ICP research and list building, refined as results come in.
---
* Personalisation
* Highest per message, but low volume.
* Personalised at a larger scale using AI agents, with a team checking quality.
---
* Account risk
* Low if you stay well under LinkedIn's limits.
* Depends on the provider. Ask how volume is limited; careless automation can get an account restricted.
---
* Learning speed
* Fast first-hand feedback on objections and replies.
* Feedback arrives as reports and reply data, one step removed.
---
* Control and voice
* Complete.
* Shared: content and messaging are written from your input and approved by you.
---
* What you pay
* Your own time, which is the opportunity cost.
* A service fee for the work of several roles.
---
* Best fit
* Validating your ICP and message; a short list of accounts you already know.
* A proven message and a founder or team with no time to run it consistently.
{% /table %}

This is Myntmore's own assessment. We sell LinkedIn outreach, so we have an interest in the answer; the rows describe trade-offs, not guarantees.

## Which one fits you

1. **Early-stage founder still finding the message:** Prospect yourself first. The replies you read personally are the cheapest market research you will get, and you will brief an agency far better afterwards. If you prefer to train your team with guided templates, our [DIY outbound frameworks](/services/do-it-yourself) offer a structured middle path.
2. **Founder with a message that works and no time:** This is the case an agency is built for. Hand over the process you have proven and keep the weekly conversation about strategy.
3. **A small list of accounts you already know by name:** Manual is usually enough. Volume and automation add little when you can write to every account personally.
4. **A team that already has sellers prospecting:** Fix the process and the message before adding capacity. More activity on a weak message only produces more silence.

A sensible middle path is to run the process yourself for a few weeks, write down what worked, and use that as the brief. Our guide on 58 meetings from 600 LinkedIn connections lays out that process step by step.
